    {'heading': 'Lesson XXXVI'},
    {'para': '**392. Verbs. Class IV. B. ⟨u⟩-class.** The few roots (only six) of this sub-class end already in ⟨n⟩ — except one, ⟨kṛ⟩, of considerable irregularity — and so add only ⟨u⟩ as class-sign. The inflection is quite that of the ⟨nu⟩-class, the ⟨u⟩ being gunated in the strong forms, and dropped (optionally, but in fact nearly always) before ⟨v⟩ and ⟨m⟩ of 1st dual and plural.'},
    {'para': '**393.** Thus ⟨tan⟩, ‘stretch’, makes ⟨tanomi⟩, ⟨tanoṣi⟩, etc.; 1st du. ⟨tanvas⟩ (or ⟨tanuvas⟩), 1st pl. ⟨tanmas⟩ (or ⟨tanumas⟩); mid. ⟨tanve⟩, ⟨tanvahe⟩, ⟨tanmahe⟩, etc. — all like a vowel-root of the ⟨nu⟩-class.'},
    {'para': '**394.** The root 1⟨kṛ⟩, ‘make’, makes the strong stem ⟨karo⟩, weak ⟨kuru⟩; the class-sign ⟨u⟩ is always dropped before ⟨v⟩ and ⟨m⟩ in 1st du. and 1st pl., and also before ⟨y⟩ of the opt. active. Thus:'},
