    {'para': 'Participle. ⟨kurvant⟩, f. ⟨kurvatī⟩; ⟨kurvāṇa⟩, f. ⟨-ā⟩.'},
    {'para': '**395.** This root sometimes assumes (or retains from a more original condition) an initial ⟨s⟩ after the prefix ⟨sam⟩; thus, ⟨saṃskaroti⟩, ⟨saṃskurute⟩, ⟨samaskurvan⟩.'},
    {'para': '**396.** The adverbial prefixes ⟨āvis⟩ and ⟨prādus⟩, ‘forth to sight’, ⟨ārāt⟩ ‘in view’; ⟨tiras⟩ ‘through’, ‘out of sight’; ⟨puras⟩ ‘in front, forward’; and the purely adverbial ⟨alam⟩ ‘enough, sufficient’, are often used with ⟨kṛ⟩, and with one or two other verbs, oftenest ⟨as⟩ ‘be’ and ⟨bhū⟩ ‘become’.'},
    {'para': '**397.** Any noun or adjective-stem is liable to be compounded with verbal forms or derivatives of the roots ⟨kṛ⟩ and ⟨bhū⟩, in the manner of a verbal prefix. If the final of the stem be an ⟨a⟩-vowel or an ⟨i⟩-vowel, it is changed to ⟨ī⟩; if an ⟨u⟩-vowel, to ⟨ū⟩. Consonantal stems take the form which they have before consonant-endings — of course with observance of the usual euphonic rules; but stems in ⟨an⟩ change those letters to ⟨ī⟩. Thus, ⟨svīkaroti⟩ ‘he makes his own’, ‘appropriates’; ⟨bhasmīkaroti⟩ (⟨bhasman⟩) ‘he changes to ashes’, i. e. ‘burns’; ⟨stambhībhavati⟩ ‘becomes a post’ (⟨stambha⟩); ⟨śucībhavati⟩ ‘becomes pure’ (⟨śuci⟩); ⟨sādhūkaroti⟩ ‘makes holy’.'},
    {'para': '**398.** The suffixes ⟨tā⟩ (f.) and ⟨tva⟩ (n.) are very extensively used to make abstract nouns meaning ‘the state or quality of being so-and-so’, from both adjectives and nouns. Thus, ⟨kṣatriyatā⟩ f., ⟨kṣatriyatva⟩ n., ‘the rank of a Kṣatriya’.'},
